    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Clarify the requirement.\n\n**How should pagination be modeled?** should define a clear client-facing contract, separate transport concerns from domain decisions, and make failures and retries explicit.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nStart with users, functional requirements, non-functional requirements, scale assumptions, state/data flow, and failure modes. Choose the smallest architecture that satisfies the constraints.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\nThe manual example exposes the core decision, state transition, lookup, or contract so you can explain the reasoning line by line in a system-design interview.\n\n```ts\ninterface ComponentContract {\n  id: string;\n  enabled: boolean;\n}\n\nfunction activeContract(\n  contracts: readonly ComponentContract[],\n  id: string,\n): ComponentContract | null {\n  for (let i = 0; i < contracts.length; i += 1) {\n    if (\n      contracts[i].id === id &&\n      contracts[i].enabled\n    ) {\n      return contracts[i];\n    }\n  }\n\n  return null;\n}\n\nconst active = activeContract([\n  { id: \"dialog\", enabled: true },\n], \"dialog\");\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\nUse native collections or platform helpers when they communicate the same architecture clearly and do not hide an important trade-off.\n\n```ts\nconst contracts = [\n  { id: \"dialog\", enabled: true },\n];\n\nconst active =\n  contracts.find(\n    (contract) =>\n      contract.id === \"dialog\" &&\n      contract.enabled,\n  ) ?? null;\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nUse **How should pagination be modeled?** in a component library where the public contract is stable, accessible, testable, and decoupled from internal implementation details.\n\nStep 6 — Complexity and scale:\nExplain the dominant read/write/render/network cost, the expected growth path, and where caching, partitioning, batching, pagination, or independent deployment would become useful.\n\nStep 7 — Edge cases:\nHandle empty data, partial failure, retries, stale state, unauthorized access, slow networks, large datasets, migration constraints, and rollback where relevant.\n\nStep 8 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain the boundary, the source of truth, the failure/recovery path, the observability signal, and why this design is simpler or safer than the alternatives.",
